The general-purpose VLM produced two features per (gid, survey) row, a categorical prediction pred\_morph over the three classes smooth, spiral, and edge\_on, and a binary indicator correct\_morph flagging agreement with the true label, for all 105 galaxies imaged by both DECaLS and SDSS. From these features four quantities were defined. Pair agreement, the label-agnostic concordance between surveys, is
\begin{equation}
\text{pair\_agreement} = \frac{1}{105}\sum_{i=1}^{105} \mathbb{1}(\text{pred\_decals}_i = \text{pred\_sdss}_i)
\end{equation}
Chance agreement uses the observed marginal prediction frequencies per survey,
\begin{equation}
p_{\text{chance}} = \sum_{k} p_{\text{decals}}(k)\, p_{\text{sdss}}(k)
\end{equation}
Cohen's kappa follows directly,
\begin{equation}
\kappa = \frac{p_o - p_e}{1 - p_e}
\end{equation}
with $p_o$ the observed pair agreement and $p_e$ the chance agreement. The class by agreement contingency table was tested with the chi-square statistic,
\begin{equation}
\chi^2 = \sum \frac{(O-E)^2}{E}
\end{equation}
McNemar exact binomial tests operated on the discordant cell counts n10 and n01 from paired correct/incorrect outcomes. Uncertainty on kappa was obtained by bootstrap resampling of the 105 galaxies with replacement across 5000 iterations, and a null distribution for agreement was built from 5000 permutation shuffles of the SDSS prediction vector against DECaLS.

For each of the 105 galaxies, the paired prediction vectors from DECaLS and SDSS were compared directly on the categorical labels smooth, spiral, and edge on, with no additional preprocessing, scaling, or normalization applied, since predictions were categorical outputs of the vision language model rather than continuous features requiring fitting on a training fold. Pair agreement was computed as the proportion of the 105 galaxies for which pred_morph matched between surveys, yielding the observed pair agreement value later compared against chance. Chance agreement was constructed two ways: a uniform one third baseline and an empirical marginal frequency baseline computed from the observed pred_morph marginals separately for each survey. Bootstrap resampling drew 105 galaxies with replacement across 5000 iterations to build confidence intervals on kappa and agreement, always resampling by gid so a galaxy's two paired survey rows moved together. The permutation null distribution was built from 5000 shuffles of the SDSS prediction vector against the fixed DECaLS vector, breaking the true gid pairing to simulate the no agreement condition. The class by agreement contingency table, covering all three true morphological classes (smooth, spiral, edge on) crossed with agreement or disagreement, was evaluated with the chi-square statistic comparing observed counts O to expected counts E under independence.

Statistical evaluation proceeded on exact analysis-specific samples, with no imputation across missing variables. The primary McNemar exact binomial test used the 105 gid-paired rows for which both DECaLS and SDSS predictions existed, comparing discordant correct/incorrect counts n10 and n01. The label-agnostic pair agreement metric, pair_agreement equals P(pred_decals equals pred_sdss), was computed over all 105 galaxies, yielding 0.8381 against a marginal-frequency chance level of 0.3497 and a uniform chance of one third. Significance of this agreement was assessed with an exact binomial test of the observed agreement count against marginal chance, and with a permutation test built from 5000 shuffles of the SDSS prediction vector against the fixed DECaLS vector, breaking true gid pairing to simulate the no agreement condition, yielding permutation p equals 0.0002. Kappa (0.751) and its confidence interval (0.638 to 0.854) were obtained via 5000-iteration bootstrap resampling, always resampling by gid so each galaxy's paired rows moved together. Sensitivity to sample size was checked by subsampling 50 to 100 percent of the 105 galaxies with 1000 bootstrap draws per fraction, tracking kappa stability. Across the 6 tests in the battery, both Bonferroni correction (alpha equals 0.0083) and the Benjamini-Hochberg false discovery rate procedure were applied.
